From 6f63a5b7de5d073474728d857e53d6eaf33dc242 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ethan Lee <ethantlee21@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 13:26:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: draw a zoomed picture with sharp glyphs from a
 zoomed glyph grid

A pinch in Macterm zooms the picture of a pane (patch 0025). This patch
draws that zoom. The font size, the grid and the pty size do not change.

Zoom.View is the zoom as the picture draws it. A zoomed cell is a whole
number of pixels, round(scale x cell) on each axis, so that box drawing
and block glyphs tile with no gap. The grid's first cell draws on a
whole pixel. The surface hit tests with the same View, so the pointer
and the picture always agree.

The renderer keeps the layout and the shaping on its font grid. For a
zoom it adds a second grid, at the font size times the scale, with the
zoomed cell size as a metric override. That grid renders the glyphs,
the underline, strikethrough and overline sprites, and the cursor
sprite. Its faces are those of the base grid in the same order
(Collection.initMirror), so a font index from the shaper names the same
face in both. A face that the base finds later is copied when its index
is first rendered. A codepoint (the lock cursor, preedit) is found on
the base grid. A zoom back to 1x frees the grid.

The Metal shaders take two new uniforms. zoom moves each cell's corner,
and the text vertex shader adds the glyph quad in zoomed pixels. Every
fragment that reads its position goes through screen_grid, the inverse:
the cell background, the cursor and trail coverage, the region-scroll
clip and the smooth-scroll clip. A shaper offset is in base pixels, so
it is scaled to the zoomed glyph's pixels. The antialiased edge of the cursor,
the comet and the trail stays one screen pixel wide. Images zoom at
their own resolution. If the zoomed grid cannot be built, the base
glyphs draw scaled with linear sampling (soft, not blocky).

At 1x every new term is an identity (scale 1, offset 0). That is read
from the source; no frame was compared against the previous build.

The GLSL shaders do not change. An OpenGL renderer draws at 1x, but the
surface still applies the zoom to the pointer. Macterm draws with Metal
only.
